{
  "dom_id": "step-q46814",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both.",
  "worked_solution": "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
